        <div id="do-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Do You Need a Compact Tractor or Just a Bigger Mower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You need a tractor only if your land has regular work beyond mowing. A compact tractor is a do-everything machine: besides mowing, it lifts, digs, grades, tills, hauls and clears snow, running implements through a front loader, a three-point hitch and a power take-off. When a property needs those jobs done, one tractor stands in for several machines and a good deal of hired labor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When Is a Mower the Better Buy?</h3>

          <p>
            If mowing is most of the work, a tractor is frequently the wrong purchase. A lawn tractor or zero-turn cuts grass faster, costs far less and takes up less storage. See our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding mower guide</a> and <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">zero-turn guide</a> for those. Slopes and banks near the house that are too steep for any rider are safer with a rear-drive <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled walk-behind</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which Jobs Justify a Tractor?</h3>

          <p>
            The case for a tractor builds when the list includes moving gravel, mulch or soil; grading a gravel driveway; brush-hogging pasture or overgrown fields; tilling a big garden; shifting firewood or round bales; digging trenches and holes; or plowing out a long driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Broadly, owners of two acres or more with a mix of those jobs tend to find a sub-compact worth the money. Owners of five to twenty acres with pasture, woodland or heavy ground work usually need a full compact.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Size the Tractor to Your Land?</h3>

          <p>
            List the heaviest job you expect in a normal year, not the one you do most often. That job sets the smallest tractor that will satisfy you. Any tractor can mow; lifting a round bale or dragging a box blade through packed gravel is a different matter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Should You Rent Before You Buy?</h3>

          <p>
            Renting deserves a look first. Plenty of equipment rental yards hire out compact tractors and mini excavators by the day or the weekend. If most of your list is a one-off project, such as building a driveway or clearing a lot, renting for the project and buying a mower for routine upkeep can cost much less than owning a tractor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size-classes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is the Difference Between a Sub-Compact and a Compact Tractor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size, weight and the jobs each can take on. <strong>Sub-compact tractors</strong> are the smallest class: engines usually sit in the low-to-mid 20s of horsepower, the machines weigh well under 2,000 pounds before attachments, and many accept a drive-over mid-mount mower deck. They are nimble around a house and lawn and fit in an ordinary garage bay.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Compact tractors</strong> are bigger and heavier, with engines from the mid-20s up to about 60 horsepower. They have stouter frames, larger loaders and a standard Category 1 three-point hitch that accepts a wider range of implements. They take on tougher ground work but are less agile for mowing and often need more storage room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which Class Fits Your Acreage?</h3>

          <p>
            Sub-compacts suit roughly two to five acres of mowing, light loader work and snow clearing. Compacts suit five to twenty acres with pasture, gardens, firewood and heavier ground work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once they learn how useful a tractor is, many owners wish they had gone one size bigger. If you are torn between classes, lean toward the larger tractor unless storage space or mowing close to the house rules it out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Will It Fit Where You Store It?</h3>

          <p>
            Storage frequently settles the question. A sub-compact with the loader taken off fits in many garage bays, while a compact with a loader usually calls for a pole barn or its own shed. Measure the door height against the tractor with its ROPS raised, because the roll bar is often the highest point on the machine.
          </p>

