        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why Does Tractor Weight Matter More Than Horsepower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because traction, not engine power, is what runs out first on most small-property jobs. Horsepower grabs the attention, but dragging a box blade through packed gravel, driving a loader bucket into a dirt pile or pulling a stump out of the ground all depend on grip, and grip depends on how much weight sits on the tires.
          </p>

          <p>
            On many jobs a light tractor spins its wheels long before it uses up its engine. That explains why two tractors with similar horsepower can feel completely different in the field when one carries several hundred pounds more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Does Weight Affect Stability?</h3>

          <p>
            Weight also keeps a tractor steady. A heavier machine is more stable while lifting with a loader, which is why ballast, such as a weighted box on the three-point hitch or liquid-filled rear tires, is recommended for loader work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Should You Compare on the Spec Sheet?</h3>

          <p>
            Find the operating weight in each tractor's specifications and set it against the loader lift capacity and the size of the implements you plan to pull. A tractor too light for your heaviest job will frustrate you however much horsepower it carries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Add Weight?</h3>

          <p>
            Loading the rear tires is a popular permanent fix. A tire shop fills them with ballast liquid, putting several hundred pounds low on the tractor where it does the most for traction and stability. Owners who do a lot of loader work often pair loaded tires with a ballast box for the heaviest lifts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hitch-pto" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Three-Point Hitch and PTO Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You need the hitch category, hitch lift capacity and PTO horsepower that your heaviest implement calls for. The three-point hitch at the back of the tractor carries implements such as box blades, rear blades, tillers, mowers, post-hole diggers and ballast boxes. Hitch categories set the pin sizes and dimensions. Most compact tractors use Category 1, while many sub-compacts use a limited version that takes smaller implements.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Much Can the Hitch Lift?</h3>

          <p>
            Look up the lift capacity at the hitch points, since it decides how heavy an implement the tractor can raise. Heavy implements like large rotary tillers or box blades call for more lift capacity and a heavier tractor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Does the PTO Do?</h3>

          <p>
            The power take-off (PTO) sends engine power to implements such as rotary cutters, tillers and snow blowers. The rear PTO normally turns at 540 rpm. On sub-compacts, a mid PTO drives mid-mount mowers and some front snow blowers.
          </p>

          <p>
            PTO horsepower, meaning the power that actually reaches the PTO shaft, is lower than engine horsepower. Implement makers list the PTO horsepower they require, so make sure your tractor meets it for every implement you intend to run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Set Up an Implement?</h3>

          <p>
            Watch the top-link and lift-arm adjustment when hooking up. A box blade or rear blade that sits out of level, side to side or front to back, grades badly no matter how capable the tractor is. Most owners learn the right top-link length and sway-chain setting by trial and error on a short stretch of driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transmissions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hydrostatic or Gear Transmission: Which Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hydrostatic suits most small-property owners. Most sub-compacts and many compacts use a hydrostatic transmission driven by foot pedals for forward and reverse. It is easy to learn, ideal for loader work where you change direction constantly, and smooth for mowing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When Does a Gear Drive Make Sense?</h3>

          <p>
            Gear transmissions, offered on some compact tractors, transfer power to the ground and to implements more efficiently. They shine on long, steady work such as tilling or brush-hogging big fields, but they demand clutching and shifting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loader work and ease of use make hydrostatic the better pick for most small-property owners. Owners with large fields and plenty of PTO work may lean toward a gear drive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Are Range Selectors and Cruise Control For?</h3>

          <p>
            Some hydrostatic tractors add a range selector with low, mid and high ranges for different tasks. Low range is for loader and ground work, high range for travel and mowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cruise control and pedal arrangements differ from brand to brand. Some hydrostatic models offer cruise for mowing and long runs, while others tie engine speed to pedal position. Try the controls at the dealer, ideally with a loader fitted, to find the setup that feels natural.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="loaders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Should You Look for in a Front Loader?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lift capacity, a quick-attach system and, if you will handle brush or logs, a third-function valve. A front loader is the attachment that makes a compact tractor most useful. It moves gravel, mulch, soil, firewood and snow, lifts heavy objects and, fitted with forks, handles pallets and bales.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Is Loader Lift Capacity Measured?</h3>

          <p>
            The spec sheet lists lift capacity, usually at the pivot pin and sometimes at a point ahead of it. The figure at the bucket edge is lower. Compare both numbers against what you actually expect to lift.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why Does a Quick-Attach Matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Quick-attach systems let you change buckets, forks and grapples in moments. The skid-steer style quick attach is common and opens the door to a huge range of attachments.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Use a Loader Safely?</h3>

          <p>
            Run the loader with rear ballast every time, as manufacturers specify. Without it the rear wheels can lift, and the tractor can pitch forward or turn unstable. Keep loads low while traveling and stay off slopes with the loader raised.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is a Third-Function Valve Worth Adding?</h3>

          <p>
            Most modern loaders come with joystick control, which lets you lift and curl the bucket together. A third-function valve adds hydraulics for a grapple or another powered attachment, and it is worth ordering at purchase if you plan to move brush, logs or rocks, because fitting one later costs more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backhoes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is a Backhoe Worth It on a Compact Tractor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes if you dig regularly, no if you dig once in a while. A backhoe turns a tractor into a small excavator for trenching, stump removal, drainage, footings and pond shaping. Sub-compact TLB packages such as the Kubota BX23S include a backhoe built for quick attachment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Does a Backhoe Add to the Cost?</h3>

          <p>
            Backhoes are pricey and often add several thousand dollars. For occasional digging, renting a mini excavator or hiring a contractor may work out cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            A backhoe also puts weight on the back of the tractor, which doubles as ballast for loader work. Plenty of TLB owners leave it mounted during loader jobs for exactly that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            With regular digging such as drainage, stumps or trenching, a backhoe can pay for itself over time. Without it, put the money toward implements you will use more often.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Deep Can a Sub-Compact Backhoe Dig?</h3>

          <p>
            Digging depth and reach appear in each backhoe's specifications, so check them against the jobs you have in mind. A sub-compact backhoe handles trenches and holes for drainage and small footings well, but it cannot stand in for a full-size excavator on major earthwork.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="implements" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What Implements Do You Need, and What Do They Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most owners start with a front loader, a box blade, a rotary cutter, a rear blade, a tiller and pallet forks, and together they add a large sum on top of the tractor. The tractor is only part of the outlay. The box blade grades driveways, the rotary cutter handles brush and pasture, the rear blade pushes snow and grades, and the tiller works the garden.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Match Implements to the Tractor?</h3>

          <p>
            Size every implement to the tractor. One that is too big or heavy strains the machine, cuts stability and may exceed the hitch lift capacity. Implement makers publish the recommended tractor horsepower and hitch category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Are Used Implements a Good Idea?</h3>

          <p>
            Secondhand implements can save real money. Box blades, rear blades and rotary cutters are simple and turn up used often. Look for cracks, worn bearings and damaged PTO shafts before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fold implements into the budget when choosing the tractor. A cheaper tractor with the right implements usually gets more done than a pricier one with none.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When Should You Buy Each Implement?</h3>

          <p>
            Time purchases to the seasons. Have a rear blade or front snow blower before the first storm, a rotary cutter for early summer, and a tiller for spring. Shopping out of season can also land better prices on new and used implements.
          </p>

