        <div id="snow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Is a Compact Tractor Good for Snow Removal?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and for owners with long driveways, snow is often the job that tips the scales toward buying one. A sub-compact can run a front-mounted snow blower off the mid PTO, a rear-mounted blower off the rear PTO, a front blade, or simply the loader bucket. Each option suits different snow conditions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Front or Rear Snow Blower?</h3>

          <p>
            A front-mounted blower lets you drive forward and watch the work, but it needs a mid PTO and a compatible mounting frame. Rear-mounted blowers are common and cheaper, yet they mean driving in reverse, which many owners find tiring on long driveways. Some tractors accept a cab or a sunshade-style enclosure that makes cold-weather work more comfortable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Blade, Bucket or Blower?</h3>

          <p>
            Blades and loader buckets handle wet, heavy snow and short driveways well, but they stack snow along the edges, which turns into a problem after a few storms. Blowers throw snow farther and keep a driveway open through a long winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Prepare a Tractor for Winter?</h3>

          <p>
            Fit tire chains or turf-to-industrial tires for grip, leave the rear ballast on, and run winter diesel or an anti-gel additive. Park the tractor where the battery stays warm enough to start reliably.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tires" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Which Tires Are Best for a Compact Tractor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Industrial tires suit most owners who mix loader work with some mowing. Compact tractors usually come with a choice of three tire types. Turf tires have a shallow tread that spares lawns and are ideal for a tractor that mows often, but they slip in mud and snow. Agricultural tires, often labeled R1, have deep lugs for maximum grip in fields and mud but chew up lawns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Industrial tires, often labeled R4, sit in between. They grip well in dirt, gravel and snow and do less damage to lawns than agricultural tires. That balance is why so many owners who use the tractor for loader work plus some mowing pick industrial tires.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Do Tires Affect Stability?</h3>

          <p>
            They affect stability and lifting as well. Wider tires spread the weight and leave shallower ruts on soft ground, and filled tires add ballast. Think about where the tractor will spend most of its hours before you choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switching tires later can be done but costs a lot, so choose carefully at purchase. Ask the dealer whether a tire-type swap can be part of the deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. How Do You Choose a Tractor Dealer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose the dealer as carefully as the brand, because with compact tractors it matters just as much. The tractor will need service, parts and possibly warranty work, and a nearby dealer with a strong service department makes ownership far easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Should You Ask a Dealer?</h3>

          <p>
            Ask about service turnaround, pickup and delivery, parts availability and how experienced the technicians are. Talk to other owners nearby about how they have been treated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does the Brand's Network Matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Deere and Kubota have wide dealer networks and strong resale value. Kioti, Massey Ferguson and LS compete on price and warranty but have thinner networks in some regions. An excellent local dealer for a less common brand can beat a distant dealer for a major one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Compare Dealer Packages?</h3>

          <p>
            Dealers often bundle the tractor, loader and implements, sometimes with promotional financing. Compare the full package price and ask about delivery, setup and training.
          </p>

          <p>
            Setup and delivery fees can add a noticeable amount, so ask about them up front. Some dealers include a walkthrough of the controls and safety features, the first service and a period of free pickup, and those extras belong in any side-by-side comparison of dealers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. How Do You Operate a Compact Tractor Safely?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Keep the ROPS up and the seat belt on, because rollovers are the leading cause of tractor-related deaths. Lower the rollover protective structure only when clearance demands it, and in that case leave the seat belt off and drive with extreme caution.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Avoid a Rollover?</h3>

          <p>
            Stay away from steep slopes, ditches and embankments. Carry loads low when traveling, put rear ballast on whenever the loader is in use, and drive slowly over uneven ground.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why Is the PTO So Dangerous?</h3>

          <p>
            A PTO shaft can cause severe injury if it catches clothing or a limb. Keep the shields in place, wear close-fitting clothes and never step over a spinning shaft. Disengage the PTO, switch off the engine and pull the key before touching an implement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Other Rules Should Every Owner Follow?</h3>

          <p>
            Allow no passengers unless the tractor has an approved seat for them, and keep children well away from a running tractor. Read the operator's manual, and think about a tractor safety course if you are new to this.
          </p>

          <p>
            Climb on and off using the steps and grab handles while facing the tractor, and never jump down. Whenever you leave the seat, even for a moment, lower the loader and implements to the ground, set the parking brake and take the key.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            15. What Maintenance Does a Compact Tractor Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Service by the hour meter, following the manufacturer's schedule. Typical items are engine oil and filter changes, fuel filter replacement, cleaning or replacing the air filter, hydraulic and transmission fluid and filter changes, and greasing the pivot points.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Often Should You Grease It?</h3>

          <p>
            Grease the fittings on the loader, front axle and three-point hitch at the hour interval your manual specifies, which is short for loader pins in daily use. Check tire pressure and look over the hydraulic hoses for leaks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Do You Look After the Diesel?</h3>

          <p>
            Diesel engines demand clean fuel. Buy fresh diesel, think about an additive in cold weather to stop gelling, and drain water from the fuel separator as the manual instructs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where and How Should You Store It?</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the tractor under cover, since sun and rain break down paint, seats and hoses. A battery maintainer keeps the battery charged through long idle spells.
          </p>

          <p>
            Log hours, fluids and filters in a simple record. It keeps you on schedule, backs up warranty claims and adds resale value. Plenty of owners jot service notes inside the toolbox lid or in a phone note.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            16. How Much Does a Compact Tractor Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expect roughly $18,000 to $30,000 for a sub-compact with a loader at the time of writing, depending on brand and options. A mid-mount mower deck and a backhoe push that figure up considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            A compact tractor with a loader typically runs about $25,000 to $45,000 or more, depending on horsepower and options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Do Implements Add?</h3>

          <p>
            Implements add thousands on top. A starter set of box blade, rotary cutter and rear blade can add several thousand dollars, and pallet forks, grapples and specialty implements add more again.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is a Used Tractor a Good Deal?</h3>

          <p>
            Buying used can save money, but have a mechanic inspect the tractor, and check the hours, maintenance records and the condition of the loader, hydraulics and transmission.
          </p>

