        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Common Mistakes With Compact Tractors
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping on horsepower instead of weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Horsepower draws the eye, but traction and stability come from weight, and on many jobs a light tractor spins its tires before it uses all its power. Pull the operating weight from the spec sheet and compare it, along with loader capacity and hitch lift capacity, against the heaviest work you expect.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the brand before the dealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                You will rely on service and parts for many years, so a strong local dealer with quick turnaround can outweigh the badge on the hood. Visit dealers, ask how long typical repairs take and talk to local owners before committing, because a service delay in the busy season gets expensive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the loader without ballast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A loader used without rear ballast can lift the back wheels off the ground and leave the tractor unstable. Add a ballast box, the backhoe or loaded rear tires as the manufacturer directs, follow the manual's ballast guidance on every lift, carry loads low and never travel with the bucket held high.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving implements out of the budget</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The tractor is just one part of the spend; box blades, rotary cutters, rear blades and forks add thousands more. Plan the implement budget alongside the tractor budget, buy only what you will really use, size each one to the tractor, and consider used implements to stretch the money further.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving with the ROPS folded down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The rollover protective structure protects you only when it is raised and the seat belt is fastened. Most tractor deaths involve rollovers, and a folded ROPS offers no protection at all. Lower it only when clearance forces you to, drive with extreme caution while it is down, and raise it again as soon as you can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            18. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which compact tractor fits most small properties?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a two-to-five-acre property, a sub-compact such as the Kubota BX23S, John Deere 1025R or Kioti CS2520 covers mowing, loader work and snow. Choose the BX23S if you need a backhoe. With five to twenty acres of pasture and heavier ground work, a full compact like the Kubota L2502 is the better match.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">At what acreage does a tractor make sense?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The jobs matter more than the acreage. Owners of two acres or more who regularly move gravel, mulch or soil, grade driveways, brush-hog or clear snow often find a sub-compact worth it. If you mostly mow, a lawn tractor or zero-turn is cheaper and faster. Write down your yearly jobs before you decide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I choose between sub-compact and compact?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A sub-compact suits smaller properties that need mowing, light loader work and snow clearing, especially where storage is tight. A compact suits larger properties with heavier ground work, bigger implements and more serious loader use. When you are on the fence, many owners advise the larger tractor, and storage space and gate widths often decide it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a compact tractor cost with implements?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the time of writing, a sub-compact with a loader typically costs about $18,000 to $30,000, and a compact with a loader roughly $25,000 to $45,000 or more. A box blade, rotary cutter and rear blade can add several thousand dollars on top. Package deals from the dealer can bring the total down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a backhoe attachment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A backhoe can pay for itself if you dig regularly, for trenching, stump removal or drainage. For occasional digging, renting a mini excavator or hiring a contractor may cost less. A backhoe also works as rear ballast during loader work. Be honest about how often you would really use it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which tractor brands hold resale value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                John Deere and Kubota usually hold their value well, helped by strong brand recognition and wide dealer networks. Kioti, Massey Ferguson and LS often cost less to buy but typically resell for less. Condition, hours and maintenance records weigh heavily on resale too, and a well-kept tractor of any brand sells more easily.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a small tractor need four-wheel drive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most small-property owners, yes. Four-wheel drive adds traction for loader work, slopes, snow and soft ground, and it is the safer choice on slopes. Most sub-compact and compact tractors sold with loaders have 4WD as standard. Two-wheel drive mostly appears on basic models meant for flat, dry mowing and light work.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

